From 3c6d8cb4d8f1e02cfdd1a64dd877c03a6079071b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Codex <codex@openai.com>
Date: Fri, 3 Jul 2026 18:20:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Add Veloce RenderPlan holder interface

Patch r25 left the speedup implementation wired directly into PDFium's
render entry points: `CPDF_RenderStatus::RenderObjectList()`,
`CPDF_RenderStatus::ProcessForm()`, and
`CPDF_ProgressiveRenderer::Continue()` all call the path-display-list backend
directly.

That shape made later experiments harder to reason about because the
renderer-facing contract and the implementation backend were the same thing.
This patch introduces a narrow `veloce_render_plan` facade between PDFium's
ordinary renderer and any Veloce acceleration backend:

* PDFium render entry points call `VeloceTryRenderPlanForHolder()`.
* The facade returns only `kNotEligible`, `kRendered`, or `kCancelled`.
* `kNotEligible` means no pixels were drawn and PDFium must continue through
  the original renderer.
* `kRendered` means the holder was fully rendered into the current device.
* `kCancelled` means the caller should stop the current render without legacy
  fallback.
* The current r25 implementation is behavior-preserving: the facade delegates
  to the existing path-display-list backend and only maps result/kind enums.

This is intentionally not a new optimization. It is the stable interface for
future ordered RenderPlan work so each later speedup can fail closed behind one
contract instead of adding more direct hooks into PDFium's baseline pipeline.

